Z||000000||shiloh187||03-26-2001||07:47 PM||||I saw someone post a picture not sure wich phorum, that they had edited with Photoshop to take away the body molding.  Why would this be hard? I am assuming it has to do with fiberglass body but I am new here so i am going to ask a stupid question..<P>cant the spots where the moldings were just be filled in with fiberglass? how does fiberglass work?  also, shaving the door off would make it the ultimate exotic look  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>shiloh||||reg||4
Z||000001||Taijiguy||03-26-2001||08:45 PM||||I considered this when doing some custom work to my 85 GT, but two problems arise:<BR>The removable molding serves as a seam for panels. The quarter panel has upper and lower sections, as well as the seams between the front and rear fascias, and the fender/quarter panel respectively. Filling that space in would prohibit the removal of the panels, short of destroying that work, should that ever become necessary. Second, the rear fascia itself has those shapes actually molded in. You would have a challenge trying to smooth them out. It would probably look slick if you could find a way to pull it off though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||shiloh187||03-26-2001||10:18 PM||||i dont know how to do this but i am thinking i might have someone teach me how to use fiberglass putty and fill these in.. i will smooth them out as best as i can do... and then have it painted, etc... anything i should pay particular caution to?<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||03-26-2001||10:28 PM||||Seeing as the molding is a seam between the upper and lower moby panels there is going to be a certain amount fo flex between there. Which means if you fill it up with 10 pounds of fiberglass and bondo it may crack later. Also look at the round molding piece underneath the GT tail-lights. There is a hug gap between that and the bumper. If that piece were to be removed there would be no-where to put fiberglass, just a huge gaping hole. And you can't leave that piece because it leads around into the side moldings which you plan on removing. I think that back piece is what has kept me from doing it. The side pieces would be pretty cake but the transition from there to the back molding looks like a pain in the ass.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||03-26-2001||11:42 PM||||I have come up with an idea as to how to do this.  People have addressed a concern of the mounting points, on the rear section of the fenders and all across the door you dont have to worry about this, only the front quarter, rear quarter and front facia to fender section.  Heres what you need to do.  On the front fascia section and rear bumper section you need to bolt it all up put a few strips of tape on the bottom of the top flange area, then do your fiberglass work, (I recommend using a 3/4 inch strip of fiberglass cloth in the channel covered by resin then use short strand fiberglass filler on top of that) pull the strips of tape off before the fiberglass work cures out it will leave a nice seam there, like the seams on todays cars at the front and rear bumper areas.  then use the same 3/4 inch strip of fiberglass on the front of the quarter panel, fiberglass it in and forget about it.  It shouldnt flex enough to crack.  Just hope you never have to replace that section, because its going to be a b1tch to fix.  Ok on the rear fascia area where the trim is just under the taillight area, do this, on the sides where it starts to hook around to the back, take an angle grinder with a 3 inch 36 disc and grind that all down until it is flush with the rest of the quarter panel.  Do your fiberglass work on that area if need be to get it all smooth.  Now under the taillight area, first take out the taillights so you dont damage them and grind down the trim so that it is flat and follows the contour of the rear bumper upwards.  then in the crevice under the trim piece fill that with fiberglass filler and taper it all in to the top of the fascia.  I think this would only work on cars with the gt front fascia.  But I guess you could just grind down the moulding on the kind of fieros with the trim that goes across the front fascia.  When you smooth any of this trim you are going to have to fabricate a body line.  You need to build up the area with filler like a point, not just flat across the trim mounting area.  Use a 18 inch file board to accomplish this.  Do it half at a time, (top and bottom) and use a string to check for straightness.  Its a hard conversion, but it will look awesome.  As far as legality goes, without the side marker lights, I dont know.  I used to work in a custom shop here locally and we shaved the marker lights on a nova and it was no big deal.  I guess as long as it looks factory original you wont get hassled by cops.  you could probably cut the marker light out of the trim piece that mounts it and make it flush with the body panels, that would look cool.  I shaved the marker lights on my firebird and installed clear marker lights from a honda prelude.  It looks pretty cool.  I think I might try to shave the moulding, to be the first.||||reg||1
